            <ArticleTitle>Acute and Chronic Low Back Pain.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>169-81</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.mcna.2015.08.015</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0025-7125(15)00153-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Low back pain is an extremely common presenting complaint that occurs in upward of 80% of persons. Treatment of an acute episode of back pain includes relative rest, activity modification, nonsteroidal anti-inflammatories, and physical therapy. Patient education is also imperative, as these patients are at risk for further future episodes of back pain. Chronic back pain (&gt;6 months' duration) develops in a small percentage of patients. Clinicians' ability to diagnose the exact pathologic source of these symptoms is severely limited, making a cure unlikely. Treatment of these patients should be supportive, the goal being to improve pain and function.</AbstractText>
